think ALES52's own design named the next rung outright — wiring the proven envelope into the limiter, compressor, and gate. That is the crux-first Lindy move: build the shared time base's first consumer, and the compressor is the most-reached-for dynamics tool, so it opens the rest.
think Crux chosen so the new module reduces to a proven one — with instant attack and release the smoothed compressor is ALES50 byte-for-byte, proven by running both tools on one signal. That ties ALES53 to ALES50 rather than re-deriving compression.
think Exposed envelope.env_step and envelope.coeff_ok pub (additive) so the compressor reads the one proven follower rather than duplicating it — TAME's no-drift discipline. Gain computed from the smoothed envelope, applied to the raw sample as m·comp/e (i64 divTrunc), comp<=e keeping the gain in (0,1] so magnitude never expands and no product overflows i64.
obs Byte-for-byte identity holds for |x| <= sample_max (every i16 but the single sample_min value, which reads at the rail through the follower's documented saturation) — stated honestly in the design and module rather than overclaimed.
obs A slower attack lets the transient through and the state carries across a split — both proven in the selftest; the two forms (from-silence and carried) share one implementation so they cannot drift.
